1
00:00:13,416 --> 00:00:20,119
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is death and what is parinibbāna?

2
00:00:22,460 --> 00:00:23,180
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a difference.

3
00:00:23,820 --> 00:00:26,281
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a big difference there.

4
00:00:28,242 --> 00:00:38,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for some people, it sounds like parinibbāna is like the real

5
00:00:39,430 --> 00:00:48,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you cannot have an objective perspective on both at the same time, because obviously it pertains to existence.

6
00:00:48,818 --> 00:01:01,708
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're either within death, from which you can't see parinibbāna, or if you transcend death as an arahant, then you can see what parinibbāna is, so to speak.

7
00:01:04,328 --> 00:01:10,532
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not... you can't just examine them as these two separate phenomena, because you are either one or the other.

8
00:01:11,252 --> 00:01:12,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Either one pertains to you or the other.

9
00:01:14,774 --> 00:01:25,620
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as the Buddha described, death is when the mind is not free from greed, aversion, delusion, and the point of view pretty much continues wandering on and re-manifesting.

10
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:26,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Saṃsara.

11
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:27,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Saṃsara, yeah.

12
00:01:28,422 --> 00:01:29,022
[SPEAKER_00]: So, death...

13
00:01:31,486 --> 00:01:40,753
[SPEAKER_00]: As commonly thought of, death is simply a wandering on, a change.

14
00:01:41,193 --> 00:01:41,854
[SPEAKER_01]: A change, yeah.

15
00:01:41,894 --> 00:01:42,554
[SPEAKER_01]: A major change.

16
00:01:42,574 --> 00:01:43,295
[SPEAKER_00]: A major change.

17
00:01:43,355 --> 00:01:46,457
[SPEAKER_00]: But not a complete difference.

18
00:01:46,897 --> 00:01:47,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah.

19
00:01:48,219 --> 00:01:57,566
[SPEAKER_01]: But the thing is, again, a person who is not free from death, a person who is not free from greed, aversion, delusion, is not free of those things because of the wrong views.

20
00:01:58,911 --> 00:02:03,832
[SPEAKER_01]: About the nature of themself, about the nature of their experience as a whole, about the nature of the world.

21
00:02:04,773 --> 00:02:10,874
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from their point of view, even the view of death will be skewed and based upon misconceptions.

22
00:02:11,694 --> 00:02:13,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like, you can say...

23
00:02:13,175 --> 00:02:15,115
[SPEAKER_01]: I can say, oh, yes, death is not the end.

24
00:02:15,696 --> 00:02:20,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people with the wrong view, when they hear that, it would sound as if, ah, so, it's not the end.

25
00:02:20,857 --> 00:02:28,059
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't even fathom what not end of death is, unless you're already starting to be free from death, free from greed, aversion, delusion.

26
00:02:29,063 --> 00:02:30,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Free from lack of perspective.

27
00:02:33,366 --> 00:02:34,706
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's wandering on.

28
00:02:35,127 --> 00:02:35,567
[SPEAKER_01]: It's death.

29
00:02:36,087 --> 00:02:43,892
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to find out where death is for you, personally, in your experience, you have to look where the things you're scared of most.

30
00:02:44,492 --> 00:02:45,332
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where death is.

31
00:02:45,873 --> 00:02:47,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Not where your idea of death is.

32
00:02:50,131 --> 00:02:52,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what people would often conflate.

33
00:02:53,014 --> 00:02:59,400
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you have this external notion of death, based on what you see and hear, and that's kind of not personal.

34
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:03,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you need to... or it's easier to not make it personal.

35
00:03:03,764 --> 00:03:09,049
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, people will develop notions that, yeah, I'm not afraid of death, everybody dies, that kind of thing.

36
00:03:09,449 --> 00:03:11,391
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not afraid of their idea of death.

37
00:03:12,415 --> 00:03:20,181
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're truly not afraid of death, which is the mother of all fears, then you wouldn't have any fear towards anything in the world.

38
00:03:20,241 --> 00:03:21,141
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is that the case?

39
00:03:21,762 --> 00:03:26,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you fear-free in regard to everything and anything?

40
00:03:26,825 --> 00:03:28,767
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, then you can say, I'm not afraid of death.

41
00:03:29,867 --> 00:03:35,872
[SPEAKER_01]: But if, oh, I'm still scared of this, I'm still worried about that, oh, you're nowhere near being free from fear of death.

42
00:03:36,844 --> 00:03:38,564
[SPEAKER_01]: There was that example that...

43
00:03:38,584 --> 00:03:41,245
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember I had a number of times...

44
00:03:41,945 --> 00:03:46,886
[SPEAKER_01]: I think they did it in the concentration camps, on Jews, they were asking.

45
00:03:46,966 --> 00:04:01,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, they were examining, like, psychological effects of the ones that were about to be killed, and they wanted to see... and they kind of came to a conclusion, if I remember correctly, that they were more afraid, people in the concentration camps, were more afraid

46
00:04:02,229 --> 00:04:15,576
[SPEAKER_01]: ...of being shunned and abandoned by their group there, by their peers, by their community, because basically, you know, that was their identity in these extreme circumstances of a concentration camp.

47
00:04:15,916 --> 00:04:18,718
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they were more afraid of that than of a gas chamber.

48
00:04:20,299 --> 00:04:27,842
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, oh, see, people are more afraid to be not accepted by their peers than they're afraid of death.

49
00:04:28,583 --> 00:04:28,743
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

50
00:04:30,186 --> 00:04:35,809
[SPEAKER_01]: They're more afraid to not be accepted by their peers than they're afraid of their ideal death.

51
00:04:35,849 --> 00:04:47,216
[SPEAKER_01]: But the actual death, existentially speaking, is where their death of their personality was, which was, oh, my community, this group that I depend on, they... basically, they dismiss me.

52
00:04:47,276 --> 00:04:47,936
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't like...

53
00:04:48,056 --> 00:04:49,137
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't exist for them anymore.

54
00:04:49,177 --> 00:04:50,138
[SPEAKER_01]: That was more frightening.

55
00:04:50,598 --> 00:04:53,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so that's where the death is, practically speaking.

56
00:04:54,620 --> 00:04:55,121
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what I mean.

57
00:04:55,141 --> 00:04:57,102
[SPEAKER_01]: You find where you're afraid,

58
00:04:58,140 --> 00:05:00,661
[SPEAKER_01]: If you follow that thread, that's where death is.

59
00:05:01,201 --> 00:05:02,641
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what death is.

60
00:05:02,681 --> 00:05:03,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Death of my reputation.

61
00:05:04,322 --> 00:05:04,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever.

62
00:05:04,762 --> 00:05:06,182
[SPEAKER_01]: For somebody, it's reputation.

63
00:05:06,242 --> 00:05:07,223
[SPEAKER_01]: For somebody, it's health.

64
00:05:07,363 --> 00:05:08,563
[SPEAKER_01]: For somebody, it's status.

65
00:05:08,603 --> 00:05:09,543
[SPEAKER_01]: For somebody, it's wealth.

66
00:05:09,944 --> 00:05:10,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

67
00:05:11,724 --> 00:05:18,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Change of an unpleasant kind that affects you existentially, again, partakes in the nature of death correctly.

68
00:05:18,446 --> 00:05:19,306
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what death is.

69
00:05:19,606 --> 00:05:21,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever threatens my identity.

70
00:05:21,427 --> 00:05:21,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

71
00:05:23,188 --> 00:05:38,675
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, if you... if somebody is reflecting and they can't seem to be afraid of death, because they're reflecting on the level of their idea of death, not on the level of what actual death is, which you would discover through first finding fear that represents it.

72
00:05:44,409 --> 00:05:46,970
[SPEAKER_00]: So, being shunned from my community or friends, or whatever?

73
00:05:47,010 --> 00:05:56,654
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, that's... that is, like, for many... like, you know, these were... this kind of experiment was done in these extreme circumstances, but it's not too different out in the world.

74
00:05:56,714 --> 00:06:02,136
[SPEAKER_01]: People are fundamentally... want to be accepted, don't want to be rejected.

75
00:06:02,797 --> 00:06:03,777
[SPEAKER_01]: They want to be approved of.

76
00:06:04,939 --> 00:06:11,906
[SPEAKER_01]: By the ones that they value, by the ones that they regard dear to them, family, whatever, friends.

77
00:06:12,927 --> 00:06:15,930
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know how much people are afraid of what other people say about me.

78
00:06:16,450 --> 00:06:20,334
[SPEAKER_01]: They might kind of pretend they don't care, but deep down you always feel it.

79
00:06:20,394 --> 00:06:27,501
[SPEAKER_01]: When somebody criticizes you, or somebody speaks badly of you, you're always touched by that and you cannot stop that.

80
00:06:28,682 --> 00:06:29,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't know how.

81
00:06:32,918 --> 00:06:33,898
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's already something.

82
00:06:33,978 --> 00:06:34,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah!

83
00:06:34,198 --> 00:06:38,139
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are afraid of the worldly winds, as the Buddha would say.

84
00:06:38,460 --> 00:06:43,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Fame, disrepute, loss, gain.

85
00:06:43,341 --> 00:06:44,101
[SPEAKER_01]: What are these things?

86
00:06:44,181 --> 00:06:45,682
[SPEAKER_01]: What do all these things have in common?

87
00:06:45,722 --> 00:06:46,342
[SPEAKER_01]: It's change.

88
00:06:46,462 --> 00:06:47,362
[SPEAKER_01]: We discussed that before.

89
00:06:48,342 --> 00:06:54,664
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are afraid of the change that affects you adversely, that it's unpleasant.

90
00:06:55,644 --> 00:06:57,425
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're afraid of suffering.

91
00:06:59,556 --> 00:07:01,157
[SPEAKER_01]: You still fear suffering.

92
00:07:02,318 --> 00:07:05,381
[SPEAKER_01]: And death is the ultimate change, the ultimate suffering.

93
00:07:06,202 --> 00:07:15,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, if you think you can still have sufferings that you're afraid of, unpleasantness that you don't like, that you want to avoid, that you fear experiencing,

94
00:07:16,470 --> 00:07:19,150
[SPEAKER_01]: And, at the same time, think, but you're not afraid of death.

95
00:07:19,531 --> 00:07:20,311
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not death.

96
00:07:20,591 --> 00:07:21,691
[SPEAKER_00]: That's contradiction.

97
00:07:21,811 --> 00:07:22,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

98
00:07:22,351 --> 00:07:24,931
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not afraid of experiencing things I don't want.

99
00:07:24,991 --> 00:07:25,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

100
00:07:26,372 --> 00:07:33,653
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the reason why death is such a proliferated idea out there for people, is because it's the most frightening thing.

101
00:07:33,753 --> 00:07:39,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, cover-up systems are pretty much deployed right away.

102
00:07:39,214 --> 00:07:45,715
[SPEAKER_01]: As soon as you start experiencing things not going your way, you start creating a buffer zone between you and the circumstances and the world.

103
00:07:48,052 --> 00:07:51,174
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, like, sometimes, you know, people get shocked by witnessing death.

104
00:07:51,274 --> 00:07:54,977
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you subject them to repetition of that, they will go numb.

105
00:07:55,417 --> 00:08:01,282
[SPEAKER_01]: They will manage to rebuild that idea, so they don't feel that that applies to them existentially.

106
00:08:01,322 --> 00:08:04,364
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the, you know, the common statement of everybody will die.

107
00:08:04,684 --> 00:08:05,625
[SPEAKER_01]: I fully understand that.

108
00:08:06,065 --> 00:08:07,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I accept my death.

109
00:08:07,867 --> 00:08:11,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you accept the theoretical aspect that you proliferated of death.

110
00:08:12,750 --> 00:08:15,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were to die next second, will you not be afraid?

111
00:08:15,952 --> 00:08:16,473
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you would?

112
00:08:17,445 --> 00:08:24,190
[SPEAKER_01]: But that idea, the acceptance of the idea on that abstract level, allows you to feel safe, while actually you're not.

113
00:08:26,271 --> 00:08:39,100
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you need... you actually need to make effort to uncover all the things that you use to clutter the unpleasant truths internally, in order to be able to start practicing mindfulness of death correctly, for example.

114
00:08:41,161 --> 00:08:43,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the whole life revolves around avoiding it, covering it up.

115
00:08:44,998 --> 00:08:48,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Cultivating only the change you want, not the change you don't want.

116
00:08:51,064 --> 00:08:55,629
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, pretending that the change that you don't want, yet you're not in control of, well, ignoring it.

117
00:08:56,449 --> 00:08:57,390
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the best you can do.

118
00:08:57,811 --> 00:09:02,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignore the fact that you are liable to death this very moment.

119
00:09:03,196 --> 00:09:04,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Not tomorrow, not in two days.

120
00:09:07,559 --> 00:09:11,423
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already like an abstract thought for an undeveloped mind.

121
00:09:12,304 --> 00:09:18,189
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's something you need to make this effort and go through this like mental drilling to keep reminding yourself until it takes place.

122
00:09:18,509 --> 00:09:19,230
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you feel it.

123
00:09:21,392 --> 00:09:22,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you feel it very much.

124
00:09:23,233 --> 00:09:27,634
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm subject to change, being affected by change.

125
00:09:28,014 --> 00:09:37,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and that's why the sense restraint needs to be developed beforehand, because sense restraint is just a pursuit of change towards pleasure or pleasant change.

126
00:09:38,956 --> 00:09:41,036
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you intentionally are accepting change.

127
00:09:41,776 --> 00:09:42,716
[SPEAKER_01]: You want change.

128
00:09:44,617 --> 00:09:49,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Just you want change in one direction, but you don't want things change in another direction.

129
00:09:49,637 --> 00:09:52,378
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as you're cultivating acceptance of change,

130
00:09:53,146 --> 00:09:59,108
[SPEAKER_01]: It means when things change in the direction you don't want it to change, you will suffer.

131
00:09:59,168 --> 00:10:01,449
[SPEAKER_01]: You are seeking change through seeking sensuality.

132
00:10:01,669 --> 00:10:04,070
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to change the state of affairs.

133
00:10:04,130 --> 00:10:08,931
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, when things change against your wishes, you will also be affected.

134
00:10:09,732 --> 00:10:18,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you are not actually mentally understanding that liability to experiencing change is a problem.

135
00:10:18,234 --> 00:10:19,495
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see change as a problem.

136
00:10:19,815 --> 00:10:21,616
[SPEAKER_01]: You only see certain type of change as a problem.

137
00:10:21,876 --> 00:10:22,756
[SPEAKER_01]: The one that you don't like.

138
00:10:23,599 --> 00:10:27,061
[SPEAKER_01]: But change in itself, you very much welcome, you very much want.

139
00:10:29,802 --> 00:10:37,525
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, it will have to happen, the times will have to occur, where you will experience change that you don't want.

140
00:10:37,865 --> 00:10:40,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Because change is fundamentally not in your control, and you know that.

141
00:10:42,487 --> 00:10:53,332
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you sense, if you practice sense restraint, just to see that the nature of change is a problem, not necessarily things change for good, things change for bad.

142
00:10:54,531 --> 00:10:55,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

143
00:10:55,371 --> 00:10:56,852
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is that things change.

144
00:10:57,753 --> 00:11:00,154
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is that change means things are not in your control.

145
00:11:00,494 --> 00:11:01,515
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already the problem.

146
00:11:02,876 --> 00:11:09,919
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to wait for the unfortunate change, for things to change to worse, in order to see their nature.

147
00:11:10,760 --> 00:11:14,022
[SPEAKER_01]: On the contrary, usually, if you wait for that, it might be too late.

148
00:11:15,242 --> 00:11:17,423
[SPEAKER_01]: So, death is the ultimate change.

149
00:11:19,765 --> 00:11:20,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Change to what?

150
00:11:20,545 --> 00:11:23,647
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't... you cannot know that, but you know this will change.

151
00:11:24,518 --> 00:11:26,760
[SPEAKER_01]: The disintegration of senses.

152
00:11:27,100 --> 00:11:27,601
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a change.

153
00:11:28,041 --> 00:11:30,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Change of the domain of sight, sound, smell, taste, touch.

154
00:11:31,524 --> 00:11:32,645
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's a core.

155
00:11:32,905 --> 00:11:33,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Change in the core.

156
00:11:34,446 --> 00:11:35,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, here you are.

157
00:11:35,948 --> 00:11:47,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Spend your whole life, before that final change of death, by cultivating, by relishing, by delighting in change towards pleasure, in change away from pain.

158
00:11:48,298 --> 00:11:51,221
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you keep yourself bound to that which is subject to change.

159
00:11:52,213 --> 00:11:55,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, when you change the way you don't want it to, you will suffer.

160
00:11:56,715 --> 00:12:00,397
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... the Buddha said, like, in brief, what is suffering?

161
00:12:00,957 --> 00:12:05,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Being united with what you don't want and being separated from what you want.

162
00:12:05,800 --> 00:12:06,140
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

163
00:12:08,081 --> 00:12:08,922
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the root.

164
00:12:09,002 --> 00:12:11,503
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the... in the core, that's dukkha, one way or the other.

165
00:12:13,184 --> 00:12:16,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Being, eh... well, attached or connected to change.

166
00:12:16,966 --> 00:12:17,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, what's in common?

167
00:12:18,406 --> 00:12:21,008
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to see what's in common to being united with what you don't want?

168
00:12:21,896 --> 00:12:23,299
[SPEAKER_01]: And being separated from what you want.

169
00:12:23,940 --> 00:12:24,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Change is in common.

170
00:12:25,042 --> 00:12:26,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Things have changed.

171
00:12:26,524 --> 00:12:28,508
[SPEAKER_01]: So you realize, okay, so can you stop the change?

172
00:12:29,650 --> 00:12:31,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you prevent things from changing?

173
00:12:31,453 --> 00:12:32,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

174
00:12:33,573 --> 00:12:34,794
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what can you do then?

175
00:12:35,114 --> 00:12:42,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I can stop delighting in pleasant change and stop trying to outrun the unpleasant change.

176
00:12:42,981 --> 00:12:45,182
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the problem is not in things changing.

177
00:12:45,643 --> 00:12:51,768
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is in you craving for a particular type of change and craving against another particular type of change.

178
00:12:52,638 --> 00:12:54,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you're bound by change.

179
00:12:55,078 --> 00:12:56,439
[SPEAKER_01]: You're under the thumb of change.

180
00:12:56,919 --> 00:13:00,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when it changes, one way or the other, you will be affected by it.

181
00:13:01,320 --> 00:13:05,640
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's just that you ignore that because sometimes you're affected by pleasure.

182
00:13:07,921 --> 00:13:13,622
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, equally, you're bound to be affected by pain, by lack of pleasure, which would be painful.

183
00:13:14,642 --> 00:13:17,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, being affected by change is the problem.

184
00:13:17,683 --> 00:13:20,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the pleasure that comes out of it or pain that comes out of it.

185
00:13:21,158 --> 00:13:26,822
[SPEAKER_01]: But as long as you only see pleasure, get pleasure, avoid pain, you're not seeing the nature of, I'm subject to change.

186
00:13:27,763 --> 00:13:28,923
[SPEAKER_00]: And the problem with that.

187
00:13:29,124 --> 00:13:30,805
[SPEAKER_01]: And the problem with that, exactly, exactly.

188
00:13:30,825 --> 00:13:31,225
[SPEAKER_00]: The dukkha.

189
00:13:31,245 --> 00:13:32,806
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see the problem of it.

190
00:13:32,926 --> 00:13:35,288
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's again, that's where these wrong views, as I said, come into play.

191
00:13:35,548 --> 00:13:36,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, everybody will die.

192
00:13:38,070 --> 00:13:45,195
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're honest, and if you don't actually, if you think about it rationally, you realize, well, everybody will die cannot apply to me dying.

193
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:53,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I can only witness other people's death externally, which will never be how I can experience my own death.

194
00:13:53,825 --> 00:13:56,667
[SPEAKER_01]: So your own death, experience of it, is always internal.

195
00:13:58,289 --> 00:14:11,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, here, the notion you're trying to apply to it, everybody will die, thus I'm not worried, is only based on the external perception, which is completely secondary to that which will be affected by death when it comes to you, your sense organs.

196
00:14:12,798 --> 00:14:25,847
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you really want to see how it would be if you were to die, imagine yourself going blind, going deaf, becoming an invalid, losing a sense of smell, touch.

197
00:14:27,087 --> 00:14:29,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, that's closer now to what death is.

198
00:14:29,109 --> 00:14:29,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

199
00:14:29,629 --> 00:14:32,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you're contemplating about the loss of sense organs.

200
00:14:33,232 --> 00:14:40,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just particular sense objects have changed, which kind of remained... keeps your assumption of sense organs remaining intact.

201
00:14:41,995 --> 00:14:43,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, keeps that assumption alive.

202
00:14:44,497 --> 00:14:46,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, everybody will die, that's why I'm not worried.

203
00:14:46,518 --> 00:14:46,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

204
00:14:47,359 --> 00:14:48,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I be worried if I go blind?

205
00:14:49,741 --> 00:14:51,362
[SPEAKER_01]: If I cannot see things anymore?

206
00:14:52,503 --> 00:14:54,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I be worried if I cannot hear things anymore?

207
00:14:55,365 --> 00:14:57,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that would be extremely unpleasant.

208
00:14:58,327 --> 00:14:58,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

209
00:14:58,788 --> 00:15:00,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're so dependent on sense objects.

210
00:15:00,909 --> 00:15:01,730
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what death is.

211
00:15:02,250 --> 00:15:05,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, death is much closer to that than what you think death is.

212
00:15:06,795 --> 00:15:12,777
[SPEAKER_01]: The fundamental change that will uproot your sense organs, not just sense objects that come and go, so to speak.

213
00:15:15,637 --> 00:15:22,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, the idea that you... that people keep trying to apply to it, is the idea based on just sense organ observation... sense object observation.

214
00:15:23,499 --> 00:15:26,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he dies, that dies, everything changes, I'll change.

215
00:15:28,121 --> 00:15:29,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, you want to contemplate change?

216
00:15:29,581 --> 00:15:33,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemplate cessation of your sense organs, while you're still alive.

217
00:15:36,612 --> 00:15:38,173
[SPEAKER_01]: And see how uncomfortable that will be.

218
00:15:38,393 --> 00:15:38,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

219
00:15:38,594 --> 00:15:39,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Will that affect you?

220
00:15:39,434 --> 00:15:39,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

221
00:15:39,894 --> 00:15:40,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

222
00:15:40,335 --> 00:15:40,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

223
00:15:41,516 --> 00:15:58,688
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're truly not affected, even at like a clear comprehending the possibility of going blind, deaf, invalid, crippled, losing your limbs, if your mind remains equanimous in face of it, okay, then you know you're practicing rightly.

224
00:16:07,565 --> 00:16:09,807
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to contemplate death, then?

225
00:16:11,048 --> 00:16:18,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Find that which scares you, that which you don't want to accept, and that's the direction death will come from.

226
00:16:18,314 --> 00:16:33,488
[SPEAKER_01]: So... That change in the most fundamental sense, that will uproot you, not come for you to witness it, which is change of sense objects, not the sense organs.

227
00:16:35,193 --> 00:16:36,854
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the big difference.

228
00:16:36,894 --> 00:16:40,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you're an observer of it, or that which has been swept by it.

229
00:16:42,679 --> 00:16:44,640
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're an observer of it, that's not the death.

230
00:16:44,720 --> 00:16:48,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't observe death, because it sweeps your powers of observation.

231
00:16:48,643 --> 00:16:51,905
[SPEAKER_01]: It comes from behind them, so to speak.

232
00:16:51,925 --> 00:16:54,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So, being subject to death means what?

233
00:16:55,288 --> 00:16:57,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Being subject to death.

234
00:16:57,710 --> 00:16:58,330
[SPEAKER_01]: You mean correctly?

235
00:16:58,490 --> 00:16:58,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

236
00:16:58,610 --> 00:17:00,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it means being subject to change that's not in your control.

237
00:17:01,212 --> 00:17:02,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but you're subject to that.

238
00:17:02,854 --> 00:17:04,655
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you're not subject to death anymore,

239
00:17:06,372 --> 00:17:08,173
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're not subject to death anymore, yeah?

240
00:17:08,193 --> 00:17:09,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the arahant is not subject to death.

241
00:17:10,593 --> 00:17:10,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

242
00:17:11,413 --> 00:17:17,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Means the change... Well, means things that are subject to change, the body, the sense organs, he has relinquished.

243
00:17:17,935 --> 00:17:19,496
[SPEAKER_01]: He has no emotional bond with.

244
00:17:19,996 --> 00:17:21,817
[SPEAKER_01]: No wrong views, misconceiving them.

245
00:17:22,217 --> 00:17:22,897
[SPEAKER_01]: No ownership.

246
00:17:23,617 --> 00:17:28,518
[SPEAKER_01]: All these different aspects of wrong views, he has uprooted.

247
00:17:29,099 --> 00:17:33,500
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when those things change, he will remain unaffected, because he's not bound by those things.

248
00:17:36,848 --> 00:17:38,729
[SPEAKER_01]: He has abandoned them.

249
00:17:42,130 --> 00:17:45,331
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, he cannot die, so to speak.

250
00:17:45,591 --> 00:17:45,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Death?

251
00:17:45,891 --> 00:17:46,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

252
00:17:46,811 --> 00:17:50,413
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point of view, parinibbāna is even more than death.

253
00:17:52,213 --> 00:17:54,054
[SPEAKER_00]: We spoke about that before.

254
00:17:54,834 --> 00:18:05,938
[SPEAKER_01]: So, death means, basically, you will still be taking up things, one way or the other, because you haven't uprooted that wrong view in regard to the nature of change, in regard to the nature of ownership.

255
00:18:09,136 --> 00:18:11,298
[SPEAKER_01]: But an arahant has uprooted all of that.

256
00:18:11,758 --> 00:18:17,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So when his sense organs disintegrate, there is truly the inconceivable end, so to speak.

257
00:18:17,903 --> 00:18:21,806
[SPEAKER_01]: So the Buddha would only, when he would speak about the arahant, he would say his aggregates break apart.

258
00:18:22,987 --> 00:18:26,390
[SPEAKER_01]: For somebody who is not an arahant, aggregates don't break apart.

259
00:18:27,110 --> 00:18:28,091
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what saṃsara is.

260
00:18:29,192 --> 00:18:34,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Bound by aggregates that you are misunderstanding, that you're misconceiving, that you're owning.

261
00:18:36,868 --> 00:18:37,508
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said.

262
00:18:37,528 --> 00:18:42,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you remember the previous life, 500 previous lives, all you remember is these five aggregates that you have right now.

263
00:18:43,391 --> 00:18:44,392
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's your framework.

264
00:18:45,192 --> 00:18:50,235
[SPEAKER_01]: For an arahant, that framework disintegrates because he's not picking it up, because he has uprooted the ownership of it.

265
00:18:50,997 --> 00:18:51,937
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, that's what I mean.

266
00:18:51,957 --> 00:18:54,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Parinibbāna is then truly the disintegration.

267
00:18:54,518 --> 00:18:57,518
[SPEAKER_00]: The death of... death, kind of... death of... Yeah, that's what I mean.

268
00:18:57,558 --> 00:18:58,338
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more than death.

269
00:18:58,519 --> 00:18:58,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

270
00:18:59,059 --> 00:19:00,519
[SPEAKER_01]: No more change after that, so to speak.

271
00:19:01,019 --> 00:19:05,760
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, saying no more change, kind of implies designation of what parinibbāna is.

272
00:19:05,800 --> 00:19:08,240
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's meaning... That can only be true theoretically.

273
00:19:08,380 --> 00:19:12,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically speaking, as I said, you either partake in death or deathless.

274
00:19:13,341 --> 00:19:14,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Parinibbāna.

275
00:19:14,562 --> 00:19:17,762
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't have a notion that you think you can think parinibbāna

276
00:19:21,583 --> 00:19:22,923
[SPEAKER_01]: While you're not free from death.

277
00:19:24,944 --> 00:19:27,885
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if you're free from death, then you wouldn't be thinking parinibbāna.

278
00:19:28,265 --> 00:19:36,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Your experience is that experience that's going to end in parinibbāna, when aggregates break apart.

279
00:19:38,947 --> 00:19:43,308
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why I kind of try to avoid talking about this, because for most people it becomes speculative.

280
00:19:45,017 --> 00:19:51,143
[SPEAKER_01]: They think they can start thinking about parinibbāna and death and how it relates to it as if somehow practically relevant for them, but it's not.

281
00:19:51,783 --> 00:19:58,850
[SPEAKER_01]: The only practical relevance there is find that which scares you, find that which upsets you, find that which emotionally disturbs you.

282
00:20:00,592 --> 00:20:06,958
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, and then imagine death being at the other end of that, not at the other end of whatever you observed and assumed death to be.

283
00:20:08,608 --> 00:20:10,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Being shunned by the community.

284
00:20:10,308 --> 00:20:12,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I cannot live, I cannot sleep at night.

285
00:20:12,969 --> 00:20:14,349
[SPEAKER_01]: People thinking badly of me.

286
00:20:14,389 --> 00:20:15,429
[SPEAKER_01]: People not accepting me.

287
00:20:15,849 --> 00:20:16,849
[SPEAKER_01]: People think I'm an idiot.

288
00:20:16,869 --> 00:20:17,530
[SPEAKER_01]: People think I'm a fool.

289
00:20:18,030 --> 00:20:18,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there you go.

290
00:20:19,070 --> 00:20:19,870
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what scares you.

291
00:20:20,130 --> 00:20:22,110
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where your death is.

292
00:20:22,150 --> 00:20:22,670
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what then?

293
00:20:22,710 --> 00:20:23,411
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you do then?

294
00:20:23,451 --> 00:20:24,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemplate it.

295
00:20:24,191 --> 00:20:25,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Develop strength in regard to it.

296
00:20:25,551 --> 00:20:26,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't try to outrun it.

297
00:20:26,451 --> 00:20:29,712
[SPEAKER_01]: And certainly, don't try to cover it up.

298
00:20:29,732 --> 00:20:30,912
[SPEAKER_01]: You wanna... What's the nature of it?

299
00:20:30,932 --> 00:20:35,533
[SPEAKER_01]: You wanna... And again, the only way you can see the nature of that which scares you is to stop running away from it

300
00:20:37,647 --> 00:20:39,832
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're scared of being shunned by your community.

301
00:20:40,354 --> 00:20:40,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

302
00:20:41,657 --> 00:20:47,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because it's on the gratuitous pleasure of safety that I was deriving on account of being part of a bigger thing than I am.

303
00:20:48,092 --> 00:20:50,494
[SPEAKER_01]: But were you really a part of a bigger thing?

304
00:20:50,634 --> 00:20:55,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Or were you just assuming yourself, your individual point of view, as belonging to something greater?

305
00:20:55,158 --> 00:21:00,782
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, can the community make you step outside of yourself and your own responsibility and your own point of view?

306
00:21:00,902 --> 00:21:02,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

307
00:21:02,183 --> 00:21:06,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you were literally distorting things for your own emotional gain of safety.

308
00:21:07,627 --> 00:21:10,309
[SPEAKER_01]: And now that became apparent when you looked at that which scared you.

309
00:21:11,591 --> 00:21:14,873
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're already step closer to being affected by that fear.

310
00:21:15,033 --> 00:21:15,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you can endure it.

311
00:21:18,265 --> 00:21:23,628
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the way I said, like, a wise monk who is practicing would be enduring the unpleasant words of others.

312
00:21:24,828 --> 00:21:26,409
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you endure the elements.

313
00:21:27,090 --> 00:21:28,130
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't stop that.

314
00:21:28,590 --> 00:21:31,532
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't stop the discomfort of people criticizing you.

315
00:21:32,352 --> 00:21:34,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it feels like an attack on your existence.

316
00:21:34,994 --> 00:21:36,554
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can stop being affected by it.

317
00:21:38,335 --> 00:21:40,236
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, you have no problem.

318
00:21:40,276 --> 00:21:41,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's a bit cold today.

319
00:21:41,777 --> 00:21:43,658
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, today I have people criticizing me.

320
00:21:45,259 --> 00:21:47,160
[SPEAKER_00]: But you see their criticisms clearly...

321
00:21:47,411 --> 00:21:50,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you won't lose perspective, because you're not trying to emotionally cover it up.

322
00:21:51,354 --> 00:21:55,077
[SPEAKER_01]: So you will even... you will be able to see if it's a just criticism or an unjust criticism.

323
00:21:55,398 --> 00:21:57,139
[SPEAKER_01]: And either way, you wouldn't feel affected by it.

324
00:21:57,800 --> 00:21:58,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Just criticism?

325
00:21:58,460 --> 00:21:58,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

326
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:02,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Like when the Buddha made those rules, people criticized like... yeah, they have a point.

327
00:22:02,703 --> 00:22:04,245
[SPEAKER_01]: It was like... he wasn't upset about it.

328
00:22:04,985 --> 00:22:06,206
[SPEAKER_01]: He was like, okay, stop doing that.

329
00:22:06,707 --> 00:22:08,148
[SPEAKER_01]: So he would just make a rule for something.

330
00:22:10,634 --> 00:22:28,782
[SPEAKER_01]: But when people don't have their perspective, even when they are criticized irrationally, people have no just basis for criticizing them, but they criticize them out of ill will or whatever foolishness they have, people will be affected and they will try to cater and appease to the people that criticize them, even if they know, oh, this is nonsense, this is simply not true.

331
00:22:30,068 --> 00:22:39,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, they're not acting from that rational recognition or, more importantly, from that ability to endure the discomfort from which you will get perspective upon it.

332
00:22:39,994 --> 00:22:48,538
[SPEAKER_01]: They're acting from that emotional need to remove the pain and have pleasure, even if it's filled completely rational, unjust and baseless criticism.

333
00:22:49,459 --> 00:22:50,740
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when people fear blame.

334
00:22:51,921 --> 00:22:53,742
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what am I fearing?

335
00:22:53,802 --> 00:22:57,144
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm fearing my rational projected idea of blame affecting me.

336
00:22:57,344 --> 00:23:00,446
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's weakness, which means now you're just cultivating your own weakness.

337
00:23:15,380 --> 00:23:17,302
[SPEAKER_00]: But seeing that, you start to uncover that.

338
00:23:17,382 --> 00:23:18,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

339
00:23:18,903 --> 00:23:21,785
[SPEAKER_01]: You start seeing it by being able to endure it.

340
00:23:21,945 --> 00:23:24,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by basically starting to endure it.

341
00:23:24,847 --> 00:23:26,428
[SPEAKER_01]: By saying, no, I will not outrun it.

342
00:23:26,509 --> 00:23:27,729
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not justify myself.

343
00:23:28,150 --> 00:23:28,770
[SPEAKER_01]: I will endure it.

344
00:23:29,891 --> 00:23:32,673
[SPEAKER_01]: You might even review your actions and you get filled with doubt.

345
00:23:32,733 --> 00:23:33,034
[SPEAKER_01]: What if?

346
00:23:33,074 --> 00:23:33,374
[SPEAKER_01]: What if?

347
00:23:33,514 --> 00:23:36,796
[SPEAKER_01]: You endure that as well.

348
00:23:36,957 --> 00:23:41,360
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the pressure will... And then that pressure, you get used to it.

349
00:23:41,620 --> 00:23:43,882
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's pressuring you less and then you get to see more.

350
00:23:46,427 --> 00:23:48,348
[SPEAKER_01]: But the problem is, pressure means look away.

351
00:23:48,788 --> 00:23:50,468
[SPEAKER_01]: You look away, you can't get to see it.

352
00:23:51,728 --> 00:23:53,849
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in order to see through that pressure, you have to endure it.

353
00:23:55,389 --> 00:23:57,470
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what contemplation of death is.

354
00:23:57,490 --> 00:24:09,693
[SPEAKER_01]: The fundamental pressure of this, in its core, my existence will change, will sweep me, will take me away like a flood.

355
00:24:13,254 --> 00:24:14,875
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to me, it's the mother of all fears.

356
00:24:16,967 --> 00:24:21,648
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm subject to change and it can come in any direction, at any point.

357
00:24:21,668 --> 00:24:26,709
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you find things that... you find little changes that scare you, that bother you.

358
00:24:27,029 --> 00:24:28,109
[SPEAKER_01]: What if people say this?

359
00:24:28,169 --> 00:24:30,589
[SPEAKER_01]: What if people say... Again, change of the environment you want.

360
00:24:32,290 --> 00:24:33,390
[SPEAKER_01]: I want people to approve of me.

361
00:24:33,410 --> 00:24:36,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, now you're experiencing change towards people disapproving of you.

362
00:24:37,551 --> 00:24:38,871
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's bothering you a lot.

363
00:24:38,931 --> 00:24:41,071
[SPEAKER_00]: I want these kind of pleasures.

364
00:24:41,091 --> 00:24:42,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Fundamentally, exactly.

365
00:24:42,431 --> 00:24:45,052
[SPEAKER_01]: All you want is to avoid pain and experience pleasure.

366
00:24:46,151 --> 00:24:56,622
[SPEAKER_01]: But you will recognize, if you stop blindly trying to outrun it, that this discomfort of that fundamental change of death is unavoidable.

367
00:24:56,722 --> 00:25:03,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing you do in this life, on account of sense-sense objects, can uproot that, because everything is result of it.

368
00:25:05,065 --> 00:25:12,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what the Buddha realized when, oh, I'm subject to death, means, yeah, I can live in a palace, protected for the rest of my life, have family, children, graduates, all of it, all of it, all of it.

369
00:25:13,090 --> 00:25:20,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, relationship between me and my own death, that fundamental change, is just fully, remains unchanged.

370
00:25:20,914 --> 00:25:26,078
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, I'm still equally liable to it and nothing I do in the world can come in between and protect me from it.

371
00:25:27,598 --> 00:25:33,322
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then he realized that the only way to protect yourself from that fundamental change is to abandon the world, the ownership of it.

372
00:25:34,886 --> 00:25:36,327
[SPEAKER_01]: The ownership of the sense organs.

373
00:25:37,668 --> 00:25:38,709
[SPEAKER_00]: The lighting in the world.

374
00:25:39,029 --> 00:25:45,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, yeah, is assumed, the ownership of the sense organs is assumed through the lighting in sense objects.

375
00:25:45,354 --> 00:25:46,755
[SPEAKER_01]: You imply ownership of organs.

376
00:25:46,895 --> 00:25:48,696
[SPEAKER_00]: We're talking about anicca, basically.

377
00:25:48,716 --> 00:25:49,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what change is, yeah.

378
00:25:50,157 --> 00:25:51,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Change means anicca.

379
00:25:51,639 --> 00:25:55,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Death, contemplation of death, contemplation of anicca.

380
00:25:55,421 --> 00:25:56,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Anicca doesn't mean end.

381
00:25:57,203 --> 00:25:57,883
[SPEAKER_01]: It means change.

382
00:26:00,085 --> 00:26:04,008
[SPEAKER_01]: If it were to mean end, all you have to do is die and become an arahant, parinibbāna.

383
00:26:05,278 --> 00:26:09,091
[SPEAKER_01]: And sure enough, there were wrong views like that back in India when the Buddha was describing them in the suttas.

384
00:26:09,572 --> 00:26:11,479
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, anicca means subject to change.

385
00:26:12,143 --> 00:26:23,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Perpetual change in this life, next life, in previous life, means you're within, basically, the framework, within the container of things that change against you.

386
00:26:24,294 --> 00:26:28,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes that against you is felt pleasantly, sometimes that against you is felt unpleasantly.

387
00:26:28,879 --> 00:26:30,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you are subjected to it.

388
00:26:31,401 --> 00:26:34,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Like being swept in a flood.

389
00:26:36,733 --> 00:26:38,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes you bump into things, it hurts.

390
00:26:38,574 --> 00:26:40,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes you enjoy the ride on the wave.

391
00:26:40,415 --> 00:26:42,176
[SPEAKER_01]: But either way, you are swept.

392
00:26:43,357 --> 00:26:45,518
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no stable ground.

393
00:26:46,679 --> 00:26:47,679
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the problem.

394
00:26:47,739 --> 00:26:51,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Not that, oh, I avoid these rocks and I go on these pleasant waves.

395
00:26:51,682 --> 00:26:51,802
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

396
00:26:51,822 --> 00:26:53,643
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that you have... you cannot stop it.

397
00:26:54,003 --> 00:26:55,804
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, that's... that's what change is.

398
00:26:55,844 --> 00:27:01,167
[SPEAKER_01]: The fundamental non-control that... that affects you.

